    "description": "Northstar Market's back-office application has a Supplier Administration screen and one web endpoint for changing a supplier's contact details. For each request, the implementation must load the supplier, reject a blank or malformed email address, apply the change, write an audit record, and commit both writes together; if either write fails, neither may remain. The operation has no cross-request calculations, no state-dependent lifecycle, and no separate batch or integration client. The current controller contains roughly ten straight-line steps, and the team wants their order to remain visible to the next maintainer without introducing behavior-bearing supplier types or indirection for a one-screen application. The decision is whether this request should remain one top-to-bottom procedure or whether the surrounding business structure warrants additional organizational machinery.",
    "decision_statement": "Choose whether the supplier contact update should be implemented as one visible, atomic request procedure or split into additional business objects and an application boundary that have no other client or complex rule to justify them.",
    "notes": "transaction-script primary: a single-client, straight-line, atomic request procedure with no lifecycle and no second client; service-layer is the application-boundary near-miss the decision statement weighs."
